('generate_with_fc', '{"args":[],"func":"generate_with_fc","kwargs":{"functions":[],"messages":[{"content":"# Task: Design NEW Positions for a Simulated World\\n\\nYou are the world designer creating **new** positions (jobs/roles) for characters in a simulated world.\\n\\n## World Setting\\n\\n## Bremer Apartment\\nBremer Apartment stands on a quiet street in Greenwich Village, Manhattan. It is a six-story brick building built in the 1920s. All kinds of people live in the building: actors, lawyers, students, musicians, waiters, programmers, and immigrant families. They come from different states and different countries, yet somehow they end up sharing the same staircase, the same hallway, and the same thin apartment walls. In New York, buildings like this often witness the same quiet transformation: strangers slowly become friends, friends become lovers, and lovers eventually become neighbors who can no longer avoid each other.\\n\\n## Familiar Places in the Building\\n1. The Rooftop (Sixth Floor)\\nAn old water tower stands on the roof. On summer nights, tenants bring beer and folding chairs upstairs and sit together under the warm city sky.\\n2. The Corner Coffee Shop\\nMost residents visit it almost every day. It feels like the apartment\'s second living room.\\n\\n## What the Building Means\\n\\nTo New York City, this building is just one apartment among thousands.\\nBut to the people who live here, it represents:\\nthe confusion of youth\\nthe trial and error of love\\nthe companionship of friends\\nthose first uncertain years when life is just beginning\\nMany years later, the residents may move away.\\n\\n## Characters in This World\\n\\n- **Aaron Whitfield** (Age: 30)\\n - Top Skills: research: 290, journalism: 250, interviewing: 210\\n - Current Position: Self-employed (contributes to The Atlantic, New York Magazine, various outlets)/Freelance journalist and novelist\\n - Brief: Aaron is a freelance journalist and aspiring novelist who moved to Bremer Apartment in 2019. He\'s working on a debut novel about immigrant families in New York while scraping together income from maga ... (156 more chars omitted)\\n- **Adrian Morales** (Age: 26)\\n - Top Skills: Spanish: 160, dialogue_craft: 145, playwriting: 135\\n - Current Position: Chez Lulu (French-American Bistro)/Bartender / Playwright\\n - Brief: Adrian is a playwright and bartender at Chez Lulu who moved into Bremer Apartment in late 2019. He\'s talented, self-critical, and struggling to finish his first full-length play. He had developed feel ... (110 more chars omitted)\\n- **Adrian Vale** (Age: 26)\\n - Top Skills: literary_analysis: 240, financial_literacy: 190, social_navigation: 185\\n - Current Position: The Corner Coffee Shop, Bremer Apartment/Part-time barista\\n - Brief: Adrian is the estranged youngest son of Marcus Vale, a legendary investment banking executive on Wall Street. After a bitter falling-out with his father during his junior year at Columbia, Adrian drop ... (392 more chars omitted)\\n- **Alaric Voss** (Age: 27)\\n - Top Skills: music_theory: 270, German_language: 270, music_composition: 248\\n - Current Position: Chelsea Movement Arts Studio/Piano accompanist\\n - Brief: Alaric is a German-born composer and pianist who moved to New York in 2018 on a composition fellowship at NYU that has since expired. He lives on the fourth floor of Bremer Apartment, works as a piano ... (229 more chars omitted)\\n- **Alessandro Vieri** (Age: 35)\\n - Top Skills: negotiation: 252, client_relationship_management: 252, public_speaking: 217\\n - Current Position: Ferrari of Manhattan (Official Dealership, Park Avenue)/Senior Sales Consultant\\n - Brief: Alessandro is a record-breaking Ferrari salesman at Manhattan\'s flagship dealership who moved into Bremer Apartment\'s fifth floor in 2017, drawn by Greenwich Village\'s character and a rent-stabilized ... (189 more chars omitted)\\n- **Alex Medina** (Age: 24)\\n - Top Skills: critical_thinking: 240, time_management: 240, legal_writing: 200\\n - Current Position: NYU School of Law / Environmental Justice Clinic/Law Student / Research Assistant\\n - Brief: Alex is a second-year law student at NYU and part-time barista at the corner coffee shop beneath Bremer Apartment, where she also lives in a cramped studio on the second floor. She is fiercely indepen ... (157 more chars omitted)\\n- **Alice Whitfield** (Age: 26)\\n - Top Skills: constitutional_law: 260, research: 240, legal_writing: 235\\n - Current Position: NYU School of Law, Class of 2022/Law student / freelance contract reviewer / tutor\\n - Brief: Alice is a second-year law student at NYU living on the fourth floor of Bremer Apartment. She is intellectually rigorous and outwardly composed, but privately wrestling with whether the legal career h ... (151 more chars omitted)\\n- **Amara Okafor** (Age: 26)\\n - Top Skills: public_speaking: 230, customer_service: 210, acting: 170\\n - Current Position: Corner Coffee Shop (employment) / Independent theater scene (artistic work)/Waitress / Aspiring Actress\\n - Brief: Amara is a Nigerian-American aspiring actress who waitresses at the corner coffee shop to pay rent. She\'s caught between her parents\' dream of her becoming a pharmacist and her own consuming need to p ... (121 more chars omitted)\\n\\n## Existing Positions (For Reference)\\n\\nThe following positions already exist and will be automatically included:\\n\\n- **Chelsea Movement Arts Studio/Piano accompanist** (type=work): held by [Alaric Voss]\\n- **Chez Lulu (French-American Bistro)/Bartender / Playwright** (type=work): held by [Adrian Morales]\\n- **Corner Coffee Shop (employment) / Independent theater scene (artistic work)/Waitress / Aspiring Actress** (type=work): held by [Amara Okafor]\\n- **Ferrari of Manhattan (Official Dealership, Park Avenue)/Senior Sales Consultant** (type=work): held by [Alessandro Vieri]\\n- **NYU School of Law / Environmental Justice Clinic/Law Student / Research Assistant** (type=mixed): held by [Alex Medina]\\n- **NYU School of Law, Class of 2022/Law student / freelance contract reviewer / tutor** (type=non-work): held by [Alice Whitfield]\\n- **Self-employed (contributes to The Atlantic, New York Magazine, various outlets)/Freelance journalist and novelist** (type=work): held by [Aaron Whitfield]\\n- **The Corner Coffee Shop, Bremer Apartment/Part-time barista** (type=work): held by [Adrian Vale]\\n\\nYour task is to design **NEW positions only**. The system will merge these existing positions automatically.\\n\\n## Age Reference\\n\\nMost characters in this world are between 16 and 16 years old, but some positions (e.g., teachers, staff) may require older ages.\\n\\n## Task\\n\\nDesign positions (job types/roles) for this world.\\n\\n**Constraints**:\\n- **Number of position types**: You must create between **10** and **10** distinct positions\\n- **Total capacity** (sum of all positions\' capacity): must be between 8 and 12\\n\\nRequirements:\\n1. **Position count**: Create at least 10 distinct position types (no more than 10)\\n2. **Fit the world setting**: Positions should make sense in this world\\n3. **Sufficient total capacity**: The sum of all positions\' capacity must be at least 8\\n4. **Vary in requirements**: Different positions should have different skill/age requirements\\n5. **Include both work and non-work**: Work positions have income; non-work (e.g., student) may also have income (e.g., allowance, stipend)\\n6. **Realistic capacity per position**: Some positions may have capacity=1 (e.g., principal), others may have capacity=20 (e.g., student)\\n\\n## Position Structure\\n\\nEach position should have:\\n- `organization`: Organization name (e.g., \\"Fudan High School\\")\\n- `role`: Role name within the organization (e.g., \\"English Teacher\\")\\n- **Unique ID (position_id)**: \\"organization/role\\" must be unique across all positions\\n- `type`: \\"work\\" or \\"non-work\\"\\n- `description`: Brief description of the role\\n- `weekly_income`: Income per week (100-500 for work; 0-250 for non-work)\\n- `weekly_delta_skills`: Skills gained per week (e.g., {\\"teaching\\": 5, \\"communication\\": 2})\\n- `min_age` (optional): Minimum age requirement\\n- `max_age` (optional): Maximum age requirement\\n- `min_skills` (optional): Minimum skill requirements (e.g., {\\"teaching\\": 50})\\n- `capacity`: How many agents can hold this position\\n\\n## Guidelines\\n\\n1. **Position count**: Create 10-10 distinct positions to ensure variety\\n2. **Total capacity** (sum of all capacity values) must be >= 8\\n3. **Income distribution** (IMPORTANT):\\n - Income range: 100-500 per week\\n - **Most positions should pay 100-233 per week** (entry-level, common jobs)\\n - Only a **few high-skill/high-responsibility positions** should pay 366-500 (e.g., principal, senior manager)\\n - This creates a realistic income hierarchy where reaching max income requires significant qualifications\\n4. **Non-work positions**: income range 0-250 (e.g., student allowance, stipend)\\n5. **Skill growth should be modest**: +1 to +5 per skill per week\\n6. **Age restrictions**: Most characters are aged 16-16, but some positions (e.g., teachers) may require older ages\\n7. **Capacity per work position**: Each work position\'s capacity should be <= 2 to ensure job diversity\\n8. **Organization-Role format**: Use separate `organization` and `role` fields; the combination \\"organization/role\\" must be unique\\n\\n## Position Diversity (CRITICAL)\\n\\n**Positions must reflect realistic diversity in life paths.** Design positions that represent the full spectrum of how characters might develop, based on the world setting. Consider:\\n\\n1. **Career Trajectories**: Not everyone follows the same path. Include positions for:\\n - Those pursuing further education/training\\n - Those joining established organizations/institutions\\n - Those in leadership/elite roles (rare, high-skill)\\n - Those in common/everyday roles (majority)\\n - Those in service/support roles\\n\\n2. **Socioeconomic Diversity**: Create a realistic distribution:\\n - Elite/prestigious positions (few slots, high requirements)\\n - Skilled professional positions (moderate slots)\\n - Common/entry-level positions (many slots, low requirements)\\n - The income and skill requirements should form a realistic pyramid\\n\\n3. **Organization Variety**: Characters should work in diverse types of organizations appropriate to this world setting. Avoid concentrating all positions in one or two organizations.\\n\\n4. **Skill Path Diversity**: Different positions should develop different skill combinations, allowing characters to specialize in various directions.\\n\\n5. **Encourage Competition**: For popular/elite positions, set **high entry barriers** (min_skills, min_age) and **limited slots** (low capacity). This motivates characters to continuously improve their abilities or accumulate career experience to qualify. Elite positions should feel earned, not given.\\n\\n6. **Design Aspirational Positions**: Include some **challenging positions** that most characters cannot enter initially. These serve as long-term goals that characters can aspire to, gradually building skills over time to eventually qualify. Examples: leadership roles requiring years of experience, prestigious organizations with strict skill requirements.\\n\\n7. **Support Different Life Philosophies**: Not everyone seeks elite positions. Design a range of positions that support different character motivations:\\n - **Ambitious path**: High-competition, high-reward positions for those seeking achievement\\n - **Balanced path**: Moderate positions offering stability and work-life balance\\n - **Simple path**: Low-stress positions for those prioritizing life quality over career advancement\\n\\n The position system should allow characters to thrive whether they choose to compete fiercely or pursue a quieter, fulfilling life.\\n\\n**Important**: Adapt these concepts to fit the specific world setting. A medieval fantasy world might have guilds, apprenticeships, and noble courts instead of modern institutions. A sci-fi world might have space stations, research colonies, and military academies. Design positions that are **thematically appropriate** to your world.\\n\\n## Output Format\\n\\nReturn a JSON array of positions:\\n\\n```json\\n[\\n {\\n \\"organization\\": \\"Fudan High School\\",\\n \\"role\\": \\"English Teacher\\",\\n \\"type\\": \\"work\\",\\n \\"description\\": \\"Responsible for high school English teaching\\",\\n \\"weekly_income\\": 250,\\n \\"weekly_delta_skills\\": {\\"teaching\\": 5, \\"communication\\": 2},\\n \\"min_age\\": 22,\\n \\"min_skills\\": {\\"teaching\\": 50},\\n \\"capacity\\": 2\\n },\\n {\\n \\"organization\\": \\"Fudan High School\\",\\n \\"role\\": \\"Student\\",\\n \\"type\\": \\"non-work\\",\\n \\"description\\": \\"Student studying at high school\\",\\n \\"weekly_income\\": 50,\\n \\"weekly_delta_skills\\": {\\"study\\": 10, \\"social\\": 3},\\n \\"min_age\\": 16,\\n \\"max_age\\": 18,\\n \\"capacity\\": 50\\n }\\n]\\n```\\n","role":"system"}],"min_capacity":8,"model":"ollama-local","tool_choice":"none"}}')world - INFO - Generated 10 positions [position_application.py:254]
