s been diagnosed with multiple sclerosis, and the financial strain is taking its toll without my physical presence. I should visit soon but fear that seeing her state might reinforce my sense of failure for not being there earlier.\n\nSocially, I\'ve been spending too much time alone in my studio. The rooftop gatherings have fewer people since Katie moved to Los Angeles, and while I value the solitude it provides, it\'s important for me to connect with others more meaningfully.\n\n### Weekly Planning\n\n#### Day 1 - Solo\n- Activity: Compose at least 300 measures of Act 3.\n- Why? Directly contributes to my long-term goal. The solitude will allow deep focus and creativity.\n\n#### Day 2 - Joint\n- Contact: Meet with Simon Delacroix about a possible composition project.\n- Why? Expanding my network could lead to new opportunities and collaboration, which is important for both social connection and artistic growth.\n\n#### Day 3 - Solo\n- Activity: Research and outline Act 4\'s orchestration.\n- Why? This will help me move forward with the opera in the coming weeks. It also allows reflection on my progress without overwhelming myself.\n\n#### Day 4 - Joint\n- Contact: Discuss potential revisions of "Tidewater" with Elara Straub, a classically trained violinist.\n- Why? Her perspective as another musician can offer valuable insights and may help me see the work from a different angle.\n\n#### Day 5 - Solo\n- Activity: Practice sight-reading and improvisation for upcoming dance classes to improve my skills and prepare mentally for them.\n- Why? This will ensure I stay relevant in my current job while also honing my craft. It\'s a practical skill that can enhance the quality of my work.\n\n### Living Standard\n\n<living_standard>moderate</living_standard>\n\nI\'ve chosen moderate living standards as it provides enough comfort to support my basic needs and projects, but not so much that it compromises long-term savings for unforeseen expenses or opportunities. I need this